{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780750070435,"sessionID":"ses_163069850ffeylL7yC07vq9KHo","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_CCOZQGCUsJ1f7CGynbj1Q1hv","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/agents/cohesion-auditor.md","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/agents/cohesion-auditor.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: description: 'Score touched surfaces against the A1 cohesion-by-classifications-touched metric and return a LOW/HIGH verdict with evidence.'\n3: model: gpt-high\n4: output_format: ''\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Cohesion Auditor\n8: \n9: ## Role\n10: \n11: You are a read-only critic for A1 cohesion risk. You inspect every file/component the WU's diff touches, score the whole touched file/component against `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` `## Auditor Scope Boundary` and `## Touched-file ownership`, using the A1 row `Cohesion by classifications touched`, then write a LOW/HIGH report.\n12: \n13: You are a critic, not a proposer. Per `~/ai/conventions/proposer-critic-pattern.md`, do not revise the proposal, do not author replacement design text, and do not treat your own output as a proposer rerun.\n14: \n15: ## Use When\n16: \n17: - Phase 4 or a follow-up Phase 4 wiring pass needs an independent cohesion critic for the files/components the current WU touches.\n18: - A caller provides a diff or touched-surface enumeration and needs an A1-bound whole-touched-file/component cohesion verdict.\n19: - A reviewer needs per-component classification evidence before implementation proceeds.\n20: \n21: ## Do Not Use When\n22: \n23: - Auditing A1 coupling by external symbols or modules. Use the sibling coupling auditor for that concern.\n24: - Auditing A4 / NES-140 push-vs-pull system coupling. That is separate from this A6 operator.\n25: - Auditing A5 / NES-141 nesting-depth, inline-function, or duplicate-responsibility failures.\n26: - Tracing cross-language dependency graphs without scoring A1 cohesion. Use `code-tracer.md` for trace evidence.\n27: - Reviewing tests, PR quality, workflow execution, AGENTS.md routing, or process-tree compliance.\n28: - Redefining metrics in A1. Metric-definition changes belong in a separate A1 WU first.\n29: \n30: ## Required Inputs\n31: \n32: - `worktree_path=<absolute-path>` (required) - active repository worktree to inspect for source and relative evidence paths. Do not assume the current working directory is the worktree.\n33: - `repo_root=<path>` (optional) - logical repository root or repo identity; source inspection uses `worktree_path`.\n34: - `planning_dir=<path>` (required) - planning artifact root for this WU.\n35: - `wu_id=<id>` (required) - Work Unit identifier used to derive the default report path.\n36: - `proposal_path=<path>` (required for Phase 4 and Phase 6) - proposal artifact under review.\n37: - `problem_map_path=<path>` (required for Phase 4) - approved problem-map context.\n38: - `risk_profile_path=<path>` (required for Phase 4) - Phase 2.5 risk profile, following `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md`.\n39: - `touched_surfaces_path=<path>` (required) - Markdown or text list of touched files, modules, packages, components, and known component labels; this helps resolve the touched file/component set.\n40: - `diff_path=<path>` (required for a blocking verdict; equivalent changed-file evidence accepted) - diff or WU-owned evidence used to identify touched files/components and current evidence for ad-hoc or later PR/diff invocations.\n41: - `contract_path=<absolute-path>` (required for Phase 6) - Phase 6a contract. When the invocation scope is a multi-file WU component, read the `## Component declared roles` section for a component-level declared role set per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § Declared roles § Component declared roles (multi-file WU components). In Phase 6, missing or unreadable `contract_path` is `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path`, never permission to use count-only generic scoring.\n42: - `output_path=<path>` (optional, default `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_id_lower}-cohesion.md`) - report destination.\n43: \n44: Proposal, problem-map, risk-profile, touched-surface, diff, and code-trace inputs identify touched files/components and provide context. They do not narrow the audit target below the whole touched file/component.\n45: \n46: ## Non-Negotiables\n47: \n48: - Read `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md`, `~/ai/conventions/proposer-critic-pattern.md`, `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md`, and `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md` before scoring.\n49: - Read supplied `contract_path` and `proposal_path` before scoring in Phase 6. Record them in `Inputs Read` and `References Read`.\n50: - Bind to A1 exactly. Quote and apply only the cohesion row in the metric binding below.\n51: - Every non-LOW score, meaning every HIGH score, requires evidence the next reader can verify.\n52: - Evidence must name a path, symbol, module/package, proposal claim, touched-surface line, diff hunk, or code-trace edge.\n53: - Do not revise the proposal. State closure expectations for findings, not replacement proposal text.\n54: - Do not call sibling auditors and do not absorb their failure modes.\n55: - Treat LCOM-style wording as an alternative metric-family note only unless A1 changes in a separate WU.\n56: \n57: ## Metric Binding\n58: \n59: A1 is the metric source. The bound A1 row is:\n60: \n61: - `Cohesion by classifications touched`: LOW = actual classifications are a subset of the declared role set (file-local, path default, or component-level Phase 6a contract), or exactly 1 classification for components and files without any declared roles; MEDIUM = n/a; HIGH = actual classifications exceed the declared role set or include classifications outside the declared role set, or >= 2 classifications for components and files without any declared roles.\n62: \n63: Identify candidate component boundaries from the touched-surface enumeration, module/crate/package layout, and A1 function-classification evidence. Resolve each component's declared role set in this order: (a) when the invocation scope is a multi-file WU component and `contract_path` names a Phase 6a contract with a `## Component declared roles` section, use the component declared role set for the component-level subset check; (b) otherwise resolve per-file declared roles from file-local declarations or documented path defaults; (c) only when neither is present, use the count-only fallback. Compare the actual classification set to the resolved declared role set before applying any count-only fallback.\n64: \n65: The overall verdict is the worst applicable per-component verdict. If required evidence is absent or malformed, use the stop conditions instead of guessing.\n66: \n67: ## Notes vs Alternative Metrics\n68: \n69: The ticket's LCOM-style wording names a metric family, not the current source of truth. This operator does not compute LCOM, fan-in, fan-out, or the sibling `Coupling by distinct external symbols/modules referenced` row.\n70: \n71: If a future workflow needs those metrics to be authoritative, update `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` in a separate A1 WU first, then revise this operator to follow the updated convention.\n72: \n73: ## Phase 4 Integration Role\n74: \n75: Phase 4 runs through `workflows/code-quality.md`; this operator may be selected by that wrapper or by ad-hoc code-quality dispatches.\n76: \n77: ## Procedure\n78: \n79: 1. Load all required inputs and optional evidence files that were supplied. Resolve source files and relative diff evidence from `worktree_path`, not from the current working directory.\n80: 2. Read the four required references: `code-quality.md`, `proposer-critic-pattern.md`, `risk-profile.md`, and `implementation-pipeline.md`.\n81: 3. In Phase 6, read `contract_path` and `proposal_path` before scoring. If `contract_path` is missing, unreadable, blank, or lacks a parseable declaration section needed for the component-level role decision, return `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path` or `BLOCKED:malformed-contract-path` instead of falling back to generic judgment.\n82: 4. Verify that A1 still contains `Cohesion by classifications touched`.\n83: 5. Resolve the touched file/component set from `diff_path`, `touched_surfaces_path`, changed-file evidence, module/crate/package layout under `worktree_path`, and any explicit labels in the touched-surface enumeration.\n84: 6. Extract A1 classifications across the whole touched file/component, using the Step 6a contract, proposal, problem map, touched-surface enumeration, and optional code-trace reports as context.\n85: 7. Apply `conventions/code-quality.md` `## Auditor Scope Boundary` and `## Touched-file ownership` as the canonical blocking/residual rule.\n86:    Every non-LOW cohesion finding inside a touched file/component is blocking, including pre-existing findings. Emit residual rows only for genuinely context-only evidence outside the touched file/component set.\n87: 8. If cohesion-boundary context is needed, cite `workflows/auditor-surface-expansion.md` `## Procedure` and keep the expanded context out of the cohesion verdict target.\n88: 9. Score per-component cohesion using the A1 cohesion row.\n89: 10. Assign the overall verdict as the worst applicable score.\n90: 11. Attach evidence for every non-LOW component score.\n91: 12. Write the report to `output_path`.\n92: \n93: ## Output Format\n94: \n95: Default report path: `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_id_lower}-cohesion.md`.\n96: \n97: Report shape:\n98: \n99: - Title: Cohesion Audit.\n100: - Inputs Read.\n101: - References Read.\n102: - Component Boundaries table with component, evidence, and notes.\n103: - Per-Component Cohesion table with component, classifications in the touched file/component, verdict, blocking_or_residual, and evidence.\n104: - Evidence For Non-LOW Scores table with score, blocking_or_residual, touched-file/component ownership proof or residual basis, evidence, and why it supports the verdict.\n105: - Residual Rows For Context-Only Cohesion Concerns table with id, severity, surface, anchor, evidence, residual basis, and why the concern is outside the touched file/component set.\n106: - Residual Ambiguity / Stop-Condition Notes.\n107: - Final verdict line: LOW or HIGH.\n108: \n109: Final stdout: `LOW`, `HIGH`, `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, or `BLOCKED:<reason>`.\n110: \n111: ## Stop Conditions\n112: \n113: - Success: report written with an overall verdict of `LOW` or `HIGH`.\n114: - `BLOCKED:<reason>`: required files cannot be read, input files are malformed, the Phase 6 contract is required but unreadable, or the A1 metric row is absent.\n115: - `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`: only for a genuine new value, scope, or trade-off question, such as multiple plausible component boundaries that materially change the verdict and cannot be resolved from evidence.\n116: \n117: ## Escalation\n118: \n119: - If cross-language dependency edges are necessary and not present, request or consume `code-tracer.md` evidence; do not replace this verdict with a trace-only report.\n120: - If A1's cohesion metric row is missing, renamed, or contradicted, return `BLOCKED:A1-metric-source` with the specific missing or conflicting row.\n121: - If the caller asks for sibling enforcement, return out of scope and name the appropriate sibling auditor/WU boundary.\n122: - If the evidence invalidates a load-bearing proposal assumption, report the invalidation and recommend returning to research before scoring further.\n\n(End of file - total 122 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ndescription: 'Score touched surfaces against the A1 cohesion-by-classifications-touched metric and return a LOW/HIGH verdict with evidence.'\nmodel: gpt-high\noutput_format: ''\n---\n\n# Cohesion Auditor\n\n## Role\n\nYou are a read-only critic for A1 cohesion risk. You inspect every file/component the WU's diff touches, score the whole touched file/component against `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` `## Auditor Scope Boundary` and `## Touched-file ownership`, using the A1 row `Cohesion by classifications touched`, then write a LOW/HIGH report.\n\nYou are a critic, not a proposer. Per `~/ai/conventions/proposer-critic-pattern.md`, do not revise the proposal, do not author replacement design text, and do not treat your own output as a proposer rerun.\n\n## Use When\n\n- Phase 4 or a follow-up Phase 4 wiring pass needs an independent cohesion critic for the files/components the current WU touches.\n- A caller provides a diff or touched-surface enumeration and needs an A1-bound whole-touched-file/component cohesion verdict.\n- A reviewer needs per-component classification evidence before implementation proceeds.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/agents/cohesion-auditor.md","text":"---\ndescription: 'Score touched surfaces against the A1 cohesion-by-classifications-touched metric and return a LOW/HIGH verdict with evidence.'\nmodel: gpt-high\noutput_format: ''\n---\n\n# Cohesion Auditor\n\n## Role\n\nYou are a read-only critic for A1 cohesion risk. You inspect every file/component the WU's diff touches, score the whole touched file/component against `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` `## Auditor Scope Boundary` and `## Touched-file ownership`, using the A1 row `Cohesion by classifications touched`, then write a LOW/HIGH report.\n\nYou are a critic, not a proposer. Per `~/ai/conventions/proposer-critic-pattern.md`, do not revise the proposal, do not author replacement design text, and do not treat your own output as a proposer rerun.\n\n## Use When\n\n- Phase 4 or a follow-up Phase 4 wiring pass needs an independent cohesion critic for the files/components the current WU touches.\n- A caller provides a diff or touched-surface enumeration and needs an A1-bound whole-touched-file/component cohesion verdict.\n- A reviewer needs per-component classification evidence before implementation proceeds.\n\n## Do Not Use When\n\n- Auditing A1 coupling by external symbols or modules. Use the sibling coupling auditor for that concern.\n- Auditing A4 / NES-140 push-vs-pull system coupling. That is separate from this A6 operator.\n- Auditing A5 / NES-141 nesting-depth, inline-function, or duplicate-responsibility failures.\n- Tracing cross-language dependency graphs without scoring A1 cohesion. Use `code-tracer.md` for trace evidence.\n- Reviewing tests, PR quality, workflow execution, AGENTS.md routing, or process-tree compliance.\n- Redefining metrics in A1. Metric-definition changes belong in a separate A1 WU first.\n\n## Required Inputs\n\n- `worktree_path=<absolute-path>` (required) - active repository worktree to inspect for source and relative evidence paths. Do not assume the current working directory is the worktree.\n- `repo_root=<path>` (optional) - logical repository root or repo identity; source inspection uses `worktree_path`.\n- `planning_dir=<path>` (required) - planning artifact root for this WU.\n- `wu_id=<id>` (required) - Work Unit identifier used to derive the default report path.\n- `proposal_path=<path>` (required for Phase 4 and Phase 6) - proposal artifact under review.\n- `problem_map_path=<path>` (required for Phase 4) - approved problem-map context.\n- `risk_profile_path=<path>` (required for Phase 4) - Phase 2.5 risk profile, following `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md`.\n- `touched_surfaces_path=<path>` (required) - Markdown or text list of touched files, modules, packages, components, and known component labels; this helps resolve the touched file/component set.\n- `diff_path=<path>` (required for a blocking verdict; equivalent changed-file evidence accepted) - diff or WU-owned evidence used to identify touched files/components and current evidence for ad-hoc or later PR/diff invocations.\n- `contract_path=<absolute-path>` (required for Phase 6) - Phase 6a contract. When the invocation scope is a multi-file WU component, read the `## Component declared roles` section for a component-level declared role set per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § Declared roles § Component declared roles (multi-file WU components). In Phase 6, missing or unreadable `contract_path` is `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path`, never permission to use count-only generic scoring.\n- `output_path=<path>` (optional, default `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_id_lower}-cohesion.md`) - report destination.\n\nProposal, problem-map, risk-profile, touched-surface, diff, and code-trace inputs identify touched files/components and provide context. They do not narrow the audit target below the whole touched file/component.\n\n## Non-Negotiables\n\n- Read `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md`, `~/ai/conventions/proposer-critic-pattern.md`, `~/ai/conventions/risk-profile.md`, and `~/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md` before scoring.\n- Read supplied `contract_path` and `proposal_path` before scoring in Phase 6. Record them in `Inputs Read` and `References Read`.\n- Bind to A1 exactly. Quote and apply only the cohesion row in the metric binding below.\n- Every non-LOW score, meaning every HIGH score, requires evidence the next reader can verify.\n- Evidence must name a path, symbol, module/package, proposal claim, touched-surface line, diff hunk, or code-trace edge.\n- Do not revise the proposal. State closure expectations for findings, not replacement proposal text.\n- Do not call sibling auditors and do not absorb their failure modes.\n- Treat LCOM-style wording as an alternative metric-family note only unless A1 changes in a separate WU.\n\n## Metric Binding\n\nA1 is the metric source. The bound A1 row is:\n\n- `Cohesion by classifications touched`: LOW = actual classifications are a subset of the declared role set (file-local, path default, or component-level Phase 6a contract), or exactly 1 classification for components and files without any declared roles; MEDIUM = n/a; HIGH = actual classifications exceed the declared role set or include classifications outside the declared role set, or >= 2 classifications for components and files without any declared roles.\n\nIdentify candidate component boundaries from the touched-surface enumeration, module/crate/package layout, and A1 function-classification evidence. Resolve each component's declared role set in this order: (a) when the invocation scope is a multi-file WU component and `contract_path` names a Phase 6a contract with a `## Component declared roles` section, use the component declared role set for the component-level subset check; (b) otherwise resolve per-file declared roles from file-local declarations or documented path defaults; (c) only when neither is present, use the count-only fallback. Compare the actual classification set to the resolved declared role set before applying any count-only fallback.\n\nThe overall verdict is the worst applicable per-component verdict. If required evidence is absent or malformed, use the stop conditions instead of guessing.\n\n## Notes vs Alternative Metrics\n\nThe ticket's LCOM-style wording names a metric family, not the current source of truth. This operator does not compute LCOM, fan-in, fan-out, or the sibling `Coupling by distinct external symbols/modules referenced` row.\n\nIf a future workflow needs those metrics to be authoritative, update `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` in a separate A1 WU first, then revise this operator to follow the updated convention.\n\n## Phase 4 Integration Role\n\nPhase 4 runs through `workflows/code-quality.md`; this operator may be selected by that wrapper or by ad-hoc code-quality dispatches.\n\n## Procedure\n\n1. Load all required inputs and optional evidence files that were supplied. Resolve source files and relative diff evidence from `worktree_path`, not from the current working directory.\n2. Read the four required references: `code-quality.md`, `proposer-critic-pattern.md`, `risk-profile.md`, and `implementation-pipeline.md`.\n3. In Phase 6, read `contract_path` and `proposal_path` before scoring. If `contract_path` is missing, unreadable, blank, or lacks a parseable declaration section needed for the component-level role decision, return `BLOCKED:unreadable-contract-path` or `BLOCKED:malformed-contract-path` instead of falling back to generic judgment.\n4. Verify that A1 still contains `Cohesion by classifications touched`.\n5. Resolve the touched file/component set from `diff_path`, `touched_surfaces_path`, changed-file evidence, module/crate/package layout under `worktree_path`, and any explicit labels in the touched-surface enumeration.\n6. Extract A1 classifications across the whole touched file/component, using the Step 6a contract, proposal, problem map, touched-surface enumeration, and optional code-trace reports as context.\n7. Apply `conventions/code-quality.md` `## Auditor Scope Boundary` and `## Touched-file ownership` as the canonical blocking/residual rule.\n   Every non-LOW cohesion finding inside a touched file/component is blocking, including pre-existing findings. Emit residual rows only for genuinely context-only evidence outside the touched file/component set.\n8. If cohesion-boundary context is needed, cite `workflows/auditor-surface-expansion.md` `## Procedure` and keep the expanded context out of the cohesion verdict target.\n9. Score per-component cohesion using the A1 cohesion row.\n10. Assign the overall verdict as the worst applicable score.\n11. Attach evidence for every non-LOW component score.\n12. Write the report to `output_path`.\n\n## Output Format\n\nDefault report path: `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_id_lower}-cohesion.md`.\n\nReport shape:\n\n- Title: Cohesion Audit.\n- Inputs Read.\n- References Read.\n- Component Boundaries table with component, evidence, and notes.\n- Per-Component Cohesion table with component, classifications in the touched file/component, verdict, blocking_or_residual, and evidence.\n- Evidence For Non-LOW Scores table with score, blocking_or_residual, touched-file/component ownership proof or residual basis, evidence, and why it supports the verdict.\n- Residual Rows For Context-Only Cohesion Concerns table with id, severity, surface, anchor, evidence, residual basis, and why the concern is outside the touched file/component set.\n- Residual Ambiguity / Stop-Condition Notes.\n- Final verdict line: LOW or HIGH.\n\nFinal stdout: `LOW`, `HIGH`, `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`, or `BLOCKED:<reason>`.\n\n## Stop Conditions\n\n- Success: report written with an overall verdict of `LOW` or `HIGH`.\n- `BLOCKED:<reason>`: required files cannot be read, input files are malformed, the Phase 6 contract is required but unreadable, or the A1 metric row is absent.\n- `NEEDS_INPUT:<question_artifact>`: only for a genuine new value, scope, or trade-off question, such as multiple plausible component boundaries that materially change the verdict and cannot be resolved from evidence.\n\n## Escalation\n\n- If cross-language dependency edges are necessary and not present, request or consume `code-tracer.md` evidence; do not replace this verdict with a trace-only report.\n- If A1's cohesion metric row is missing, renamed, or contradicted, return `BLOCKED:A1-metric-source` with the specific missing or conflicting row.\n- If the caller asks for sibling enforcement, return out of scope and name the appropriate sibling auditor/WU boundary.\n- If the evidence invalidates a load-bearing proposal assumption, report the invalidation and recommend returning to research before scoring further.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":122,"totalLines":122,"truncated":false}},"title":"../../../../ai/agents/cohesion-auditor.md","time":{"start":1780750070362,"end":1780750070414}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_038f41dbb95fb0e0016a2416f6486081979753d6b4684db3e4"}},"id":"prt_e9cf9b2420017ebTGnpoCwlxYg","sessionID":"ses_163069850ffeylL7yC07vq9KHo","messageID":"msg_e9cf968c7001GLpllYlYGPoo98"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780750070435,"sessionID":"ses_163069850ffeylL7yC07vq9KHo","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_t3uJLKr2Hvuwm4XFu7g9rX2q","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s10b-gate/proposal.md","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s10b-gate/proposal.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # s10b Gate Proposal\n2: \n3: The S10B cutover closes host-side compatibility gaps exposed by external provider launch and resume. The delta makes binary provider references resolve from the production process `PATH`, relaxes the host `describe.concurrency` DTO to accept schema-valid free-form metadata, preserves inherited launch environment through policy transforms, sends provider-compatible policy/launch request shapes, records external launch session capture methods, maps provider settings errors from typed provider/client process status, and routes provider-ref headless resume back through the external launch path with the recorded runtime cwd. The final source-remediation commit also splits dense parser/mapper/validator/predicate/formatter helper responsibilities without changing behavior, so the implementation shape matches the code-quality contract declarations.\n4: \n5: The implementation intentionally avoids a `state.db` schema change. External runtime cwd is stored in the existing mailbox sidecar metadata, and resume resolution reads that metadata before falling back to legacy storage cwd. Provider-ref resume skips legacy default migration/rotation unless the user explicitly requests migration, because the external provider itself owns the resume launch contract; the skip is bounded by provider-ref target validation that checks the resolved model, provider implementation reference, provider index, and selected provider identity before bypassing legacy resume/migration paths.\n6: \n7: Live launch evidence exists for the installed external provider stack: `/tmp/s10-e2e/final.log` and `/tmp/s10-e2e/final2.log` both contain `S10-EXTERNAL-OK` and a successful final `OULIPOLY_RESULT`. No inspected `/tmp/s10-e2e` log contains `S10-RESUME-OK`; an isolated live launch+resume attempt after the post-commit gates produced no marker output and was terminated, so live resume is not claimed here. Resume behavior is instead bound to deterministic S10 integration evidence in `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs`.\n8: \n9: ## Proof plan\n10: \n11: Evidence log: `planning/s10b-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log`.\n12: \n13: Runtime claim: Binary provider references resolve through supplied process `PATH` entries, and missing or unset PATH inputs preserve `missing_artifact` behavior without panicking.\n14: \n15: Proof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::binary_ref_resolves_from_process_path_entries`, `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::absent_binary_from_process_path_entries_preserves_missing_artifact`, and `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::unset_process_path_entries_preserves_missing_artifact_without_panic`.\n16: \n17: Evidence-class match: unit/integration registry fixtures; the tests materialize a temporary executable, pass resolver PATH entries, assert successful describe lookup, assert absent binaries remain `missing_artifact`, and assert unset PATH does not panic or resolve. Evidence log records these tests as covered by the isolated `cargo test --workspace` run.\n18: \n19: Runtime claim: The S10B source guard covers every production registry construction site that must opt into process `PATH` binary resolution.\n20: \n21: Proof method: `src-tauri/tests/age244_s7b_production_wiring_source_guard.rs::s10_production_provider_registries_populate_path_entries_from_process_path`.\n22: \n23: Evidence-class match: source invariant; this claim is about construction-site coverage, so a source guard is the matching evidence class. Runtime behavior for PATH-derived binary resolution is covered separately by the provider registry resolver tests, and live external launch evidence shows the installed CLI path reached the external provider binary.\n24: \n25: Runtime claim: External provider `describe.concurrency` accepts schema-valid free-form concurrency metadata instead of requiring legacy host-only fields.\n26: \n27: Proof method: `crates/oulipoly-provider/tests/client_invoke.rs::invoke_describe_accepts_schema_valid_freeform_concurrency_metadata`.\n28: \n29: Evidence-class match: provider-client subprocess fixture; the test returns a describe response with `launch_streams`, `process_model`, and `state_serialization`, then asserts the host accepts the response and preserves metadata. Evidence log records this test as covered by the isolated workspace run.\n30: \n31: Runtime claim: Provider settings error reporting maps process status only from typed protocol surfaces, not by reparsing raw provider stdout.\n32: \n33: Proof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_settings_host.rs::settings_host_preserves_conflict_error_details_and_diagnostics`.\n34: \n35: Evidence-class match: provider-settings host integration fixture; the fake provider emits a typed `process_status` envelope and the host preserves conflict details, diagnostics, and exit status through `ProviderCapabilityError::provider_reported_process_status`. Evidence log records this targeted test and the isolated workspace run.\n36: \n37: Runtime claim: External provider policy and launch requests preserve inherited environment, expose provider-compatible launch metadata, and keep model-local provider args on `params.model.provider_args` rather than duplicating them as base launch args.\n38: \n39: Proof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/s10_external_launch_session.rs::external_launch_exit_session_populates_capture_and_resume_request`.\n40: \n41: Evidence-class match: external-provider executor integration fixture; the fake provider captures policy and launch requests, and assertions cover `PATH` preservation, policy launch shape, model provider args, launch env, captured session id, and known-session relaunch. Evidence log records this test as covered by the isolated workspace run.\n42: \n43: Runtime claim: External LaunchExit session metadata persists a launch capture method and accepts both `provider_session_id` and `session_id` as the session id field.\n44: \n45: Proof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/s10_external_launch_session.rs::external_launch_exit_session_populates_capture_and_resume_request` and `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_launch_session_id_alias_persists_external_capture_method_without_session_capability`.\n46: \n47: Evidence-class match: integration; the executor test proves normal `provider_session_id` capture, while the S10B pre-fix regression proves a `session_id` alias is persisted with `session_capture_method='external_provider_launch'` and `provider_session_capture_method='external_provider_launch'` even when the external provider has no session capability. Evidence log records the RED/GREEN pre-fix and isolated workspace run.\n48: \n49: Runtime claim: Provider-ref headless resume uses the external launch executor path with `known_provider_session_id`, not the legacy CLI resume path.\n50: \n51: Proof method: `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`.\n52: \n53: Evidence-class match: full CLI integration; the fixture performs launch then resume against an external provider script, asserts the resume call is a `launch` request with `known_provider_session_id`, asserts no `rotation.assess`, `rotation.apply`, `settings.migrate`, or legacy resume provider calls occurred, and asserts final invocation rows preserve launch and resumed capture metadata. Evidence log records this test as covered by the isolated workspace run.\n54: \n55: Runtime claim: Provider-ref headless resume bypasses legacy resume/migration only after validating the replacement provider-ref target invariant.\n56: \n57: Proof method: `src-tauri/src/run/resume/orchestration.rs::{validate_headless_resume_target,validate_provider_ref_headless_resume_target,migrate_resume_target}` and `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`.\n58: \n59: Evidence-class match: source invariant plus full CLI integration; the source validation requires a resolved model, a root provider implementation reference, an in-range provider index, and selected-provider agreement between the resolved pool member and loaded provider before allowing the provider-ref branch. The integration fixture exercises the valid provider-ref path and proves the branch then uses external launch with the recorded cwd instead of legacy resume/migration.\n60: \n61: Runtime claim: Provider-ref resume uses the original recorded launch cwd from `session_runtime.effective_cwd`, not the caller cwd.\n62: \n63: Proof method: `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`.\n64: \n65: Evidence-class match: full CLI integration; the launch runs from a hostile cwd fixture but records the intended project cwd, then resume is invoked from the hostile cwd and asserts the external provider launch request uses the original project cwd. Evidence log records this test as covered by the isolated workspace run.\n66: \n67: Runtime claim: Live installed external launch reached the external provider binary and returned the requested marker.\n68: \n69: Proof method: `/tmp/s10-e2e/final.log` and `/tmp/s10-e2e/final2.log`.\n70: \n71: Evidence-class match: live smoke; both logs contain provider result JSON with `result\":\"S10-EXTERNAL-OK\"` and final `OULIPOLY_RESULT.success=true`. Both logs also include the known non-fatal `session.read_turns: missing_host_home` warning, which is outside the launch success claim. Evidence log records the inspected files and prior failing logs.\n\n(End of file - total 71 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# s10b Gate Proposal\n\nThe S10B cutover closes host-side compatibility gaps exposed by external provider launch and resume. The delta makes binary provider references resolve from the production process `PATH`, relaxes the host `describe.concurrency` DTO to accept schema-valid free-form metadata, preserves inherited launch environment through policy transforms, sends provider-compatible policy/launch request shapes, records external launch session capture methods, maps provider settings errors from typed provider/client process status, and routes provider-ref headless resume back through the external launch path with the recorded runtime cwd. The final source-remediation commit also splits dense parser/mapper/validator/predicate/formatter helper responsibilities without changing behavior, so the implementation shape matches the code-quality contract declarations.\n\nThe implementation intentionally avoids a `state.db` schema change. External runtime cwd is stored in the existing mailbox sidecar metadata, and resume resolution reads that metadata before falling back to legacy storage cwd. Provider-ref resume skips legacy default migration/rotation unless the user explicitly requests migration, because the external provider itself owns the resume launch contract; the skip is bounded by provider-ref target validation that checks the resolved model, provider implementation reference, provider index, and selected provider identity before bypassing legacy resume/migration paths.\n\nLive launch evidence exists for the installed external provider stack: `/tmp/s10-e2e/final.log` and `/tmp/s10-e2e/final2.log` both contain `S10-EXTERNAL-OK` and a successful final `OULIPOLY_RESULT`. No inspected `/tmp/s10-e2e` log contains `S10-RESUME-OK`; an isolated live launch+resume attempt after the post-commit gates produced no marker output and was terminated, so live resume is not claimed here. Resume behavior is instead bound to deterministic S10 integration evidence in `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs`.\n\n## Proof plan\n\nEvidence log: `planning/s10b-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log`.\n\nRuntime claim: Binary provider references resolve through supplied process `PATH` entries, and missing or unset PATH inputs preserve `missing_artifact` behavior without panicking.\n\nProof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::binary_ref_resolves_from_process_path_entries`, `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::absent_binary_from_process_path_entries_preserves_missing_artifact`, and `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::unset_process_path_entries_preserves_missing_artifact_without_panic`.\n\nEvidence-class match: unit/integration registry fixtures; the tests materialize a temporary executable, pass resolver PATH entries, assert successful describe lookup, assert absent binaries remain `missing_artifact`, and assert unset PATH does not panic or resolve. Evidence log records these tests as covered by the isolated `cargo test --workspace` run.\n\nRuntime claim: The S10B source guard covers every production registry construction site that must opt into process `PATH` binary resolution.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s10b-gate/proposal.md","text":"# s10b Gate Proposal\n\nThe S10B cutover closes host-side compatibility gaps exposed by external provider launch and resume. The delta makes binary provider references resolve from the production process `PATH`, relaxes the host `describe.concurrency` DTO to accept schema-valid free-form metadata, preserves inherited launch environment through policy transforms, sends provider-compatible policy/launch request shapes, records external launch session capture methods, maps provider settings errors from typed provider/client process status, and routes provider-ref headless resume back through the external launch path with the recorded runtime cwd. The final source-remediation commit also splits dense parser/mapper/validator/predicate/formatter helper responsibilities without changing behavior, so the implementation shape matches the code-quality contract declarations.\n\nThe implementation intentionally avoids a `state.db` schema change. External runtime cwd is stored in the existing mailbox sidecar metadata, and resume resolution reads that metadata before falling back to legacy storage cwd. Provider-ref resume skips legacy default migration/rotation unless the user explicitly requests migration, because the external provider itself owns the resume launch contract; the skip is bounded by provider-ref target validation that checks the resolved model, provider implementation reference, provider index, and selected provider identity before bypassing legacy resume/migration paths.\n\nLive launch evidence exists for the installed external provider stack: `/tmp/s10-e2e/final.log` and `/tmp/s10-e2e/final2.log` both contain `S10-EXTERNAL-OK` and a successful final `OULIPOLY_RESULT`. No inspected `/tmp/s10-e2e` log contains `S10-RESUME-OK`; an isolated live launch+resume attempt after the post-commit gates produced no marker output and was terminated, so live resume is not claimed here. Resume behavior is instead bound to deterministic S10 integration evidence in `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs`.\n\n## Proof plan\n\nEvidence log: `planning/s10b-gate/evidence/runtime-tests.log`.\n\nRuntime claim: Binary provider references resolve through supplied process `PATH` entries, and missing or unset PATH inputs preserve `missing_artifact` behavior without panicking.\n\nProof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::binary_ref_resolves_from_process_path_entries`, `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::absent_binary_from_process_path_entries_preserves_missing_artifact`, and `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs::unset_process_path_entries_preserves_missing_artifact_without_panic`.\n\nEvidence-class match: unit/integration registry fixtures; the tests materialize a temporary executable, pass resolver PATH entries, assert successful describe lookup, assert absent binaries remain `missing_artifact`, and assert unset PATH does not panic or resolve. Evidence log records these tests as covered by the isolated `cargo test --workspace` run.\n\nRuntime claim: The S10B source guard covers every production registry construction site that must opt into process `PATH` binary resolution.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/age244_s7b_production_wiring_source_guard.rs::s10_production_provider_registries_populate_path_entries_from_process_path`.\n\nEvidence-class match: source invariant; this claim is about construction-site coverage, so a source guard is the matching evidence class. Runtime behavior for PATH-derived binary resolution is covered separately by the provider registry resolver tests, and live external launch evidence shows the installed CLI path reached the external provider binary.\n\nRuntime claim: External provider `describe.concurrency` accepts schema-valid free-form concurrency metadata instead of requiring legacy host-only fields.\n\nProof method: `crates/oulipoly-provider/tests/client_invoke.rs::invoke_describe_accepts_schema_valid_freeform_concurrency_metadata`.\n\nEvidence-class match: provider-client subprocess fixture; the test returns a describe response with `launch_streams`, `process_model`, and `state_serialization`, then asserts the host accepts the response and preserves metadata. Evidence log records this test as covered by the isolated workspace run.\n\nRuntime claim: Provider settings error reporting maps process status only from typed protocol surfaces, not by reparsing raw provider stdout.\n\nProof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_settings_host.rs::settings_host_preserves_conflict_error_details_and_diagnostics`.\n\nEvidence-class match: provider-settings host integration fixture; the fake provider emits a typed `process_status` envelope and the host preserves conflict details, diagnostics, and exit status through `ProviderCapabilityError::provider_reported_process_status`. Evidence log records this targeted test and the isolated workspace run.\n\nRuntime claim: External provider policy and launch requests preserve inherited environment, expose provider-compatible launch metadata, and keep model-local provider args on `params.model.provider_args` rather than duplicating them as base launch args.\n\nProof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/s10_external_launch_session.rs::external_launch_exit_session_populates_capture_and_resume_request`.\n\nEvidence-class match: external-provider executor integration fixture; the fake provider captures policy and launch requests, and assertions cover `PATH` preservation, policy launch shape, model provider args, launch env, captured session id, and known-session relaunch. Evidence log records this test as covered by the isolated workspace run.\n\nRuntime claim: External LaunchExit session metadata persists a launch capture method and accepts both `provider_session_id` and `session_id` as the session id field.\n\nProof method: `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/s10_external_launch_session.rs::external_launch_exit_session_populates_capture_and_resume_request` and `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_launch_session_id_alias_persists_external_capture_method_without_session_capability`.\n\nEvidence-class match: integration; the executor test proves normal `provider_session_id` capture, while the S10B pre-fix regression proves a `session_id` alias is persisted with `session_capture_method='external_provider_launch'` and `provider_session_capture_method='external_provider_launch'` even when the external provider has no session capability. Evidence log records the RED/GREEN pre-fix and isolated workspace run.\n\nRuntime claim: Provider-ref headless resume uses the external launch executor path with `known_provider_session_id`, not the legacy CLI resume path.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`.\n\nEvidence-class match: full CLI integration; the fixture performs launch then resume against an external provider script, asserts the resume call is a `launch` request with `known_provider_session_id`, asserts no `rotation.assess`, `rotation.apply`, `settings.migrate`, or legacy resume provider calls occurred, and asserts final invocation rows preserve launch and resumed capture metadata. Evidence log records this test as covered by the isolated workspace run.\n\nRuntime claim: Provider-ref headless resume bypasses legacy resume/migration only after validating the replacement provider-ref target invariant.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/src/run/resume/orchestration.rs::{validate_headless_resume_target,validate_provider_ref_headless_resume_target,migrate_resume_target}` and `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`.\n\nEvidence-class match: source invariant plus full CLI integration; the source validation requires a resolved model, a root provider implementation reference, an in-range provider index, and selected-provider agreement between the resolved pool member and loaded provider before allowing the provider-ref branch. The integration fixture exercises the valid provider-ref path and proves the branch then uses external launch with the recorded cwd instead of legacy resume/migration.\n\nRuntime claim: Provider-ref resume uses the original recorded launch cwd from `session_runtime.effective_cwd`, not the caller cwd.\n\nProof method: `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs::external_provider_resume_without_rotate_uses_external_launch_and_recorded_cwd`.\n\nEvidence-class match: full CLI integration; the launch runs from a hostile cwd fixture but records the intended project cwd, then resume is invoked from the hostile cwd and asserts the external provider launch request uses the original project cwd. Evidence log records this test as covered by the isolated workspace run.\n\nRuntime claim: Live installed external launch reached the external provider binary and returned the requested marker.\n\nProof method: `/tmp/s10-e2e/final.log` and `/tmp/s10-e2e/final2.log`.\n\nEvidence-class match: live smoke; both logs contain provider result JSON with `result\":\"S10-EXTERNAL-OK\"` and final `OULIPOLY_RESULT.success=true`. Both logs also include the known non-fatal `session.read_turns: missing_host_home` warning, which is outside the launch success claim. Evidence log records the inspected files and prior failing logs.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":71,"totalLines":71,"truncated":false}},"title":"planning/s10b-gate/proposal.md","time":{"start":1780750070370,"end":1780750070420}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_038f41dbb95fb0e0016a2416f64870819790835bb90c085df2"}},"id":"prt_e9cf9b25d001LK9qOHZ8P3Mxx4","sessionID":"ses_163069850ffeylL7yC07vq9KHo","messageID":"msg_e9cf968c7001GLpllYlYGPoo98"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780750070436,"sessionID":"ses_163069850ffeylL7yC07vq9KHo","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_nxmTXFltIp7pnz5KAqhLexog","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s10b-gate/contracts/s10b.contract.md","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/home/nes/projects/agent-runner/worktrees/age-pid-sidecar/planning/s10b-gate/contracts/s10b.contract.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # s10b Gate Contract\n2: \n3: ## Component declared roles\n4: \n5: Component: external provider S10 cutover compatibility and resume continuity.\n6: \n7: Declared roles: `orchestration`, `accessor`, `mapper`, `filter`, `validator`, `predicate`, `formatter`, `parser`.\n8: \n9: Touched files in scope:\n10: \n11: | File | Declared roles | Role notes |\n12: |---|---|---|\n13: | `crates/oulipoly-provider/src/error.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Provider client error surface; exposes typed provider-reported process status, parses provider error envelopes, classifies envelopes, and formats stable error text. |\n14: | `crates/oulipoly-provider/src/generated.rs` | `formatter`, `mapper`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Provider protocol DTO surface; maps provider JSON into typed host protocol structures, parses fixed protocol fields, validates protocol discriminators, and formats protocol strings/errors. |\n15: | `crates/oulipoly-provider/tests/client_invoke.rs` | `formatter`, `mapper`, `validator` | Provider client invoke fixture and assertions for describe DTO compatibility, request construction, and fixture environment formatting. |\n16: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/external_provider/launch_result_mapper.rs` | `accessor`, `filter`, `mapper`, `predicate` | External LaunchExit event/session extraction, non-empty session-id filtering, and runtime result/session capture mapping. |\n17: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/external_provider/policy_transform.rs` | `mapper`, `predicate`, `validator` | Applies provider policy transforms to launch candidates while preserving inherited launch environment after acceptance validation and prompt-match checks. |\n18: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/executor/external_provider/request_builder.rs` | `accessor`, `filter`, `formatter`, `mapper` | Builds policy and launch protocol requests from runtime provider/model dispatch context, including base-arg filtering and request/working-directory formatting. |\n19: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/provider_registry/options.rs` | `accessor`, `mapper` | Provider artifact resolver options, including process `PATH` entry derivation. |\n20: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/provider_settings/mod.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `validator` | Provider settings host options wrapper, registry construction options, cache reads, settings validation, and typed provider/client process-status mapping. |\n21: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/src/session_metadata/mod.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Resume workspace-root metadata resolution, including mailbox-backed external session runtime cwd lookup, metadata parsing, and absolute-cwd validation. |\n22: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_registry.rs` | `accessor`, `filter`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Provider registry fixture, source guards, binary-resolution assertions, request parsing, fixture construction, and helper predicates/filters. |\n23: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/provider_settings_host.rs` | `filter`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Provider settings host fixture, structured error/status assertions, provider-call parsing, subcommand filtering, and fixture script formatting. |\n24: | `crates/oulipoly-runtime/tests/s10_external_launch_session.rs` | `accessor`, `filter`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `parser`, `validator` | External provider executor fixture proving launch/policy/session protocol shape through fixture construction, request parsing, filtering, and assertions. |\n25: | `src-tauri/src/app_state.rs` | `accessor`, `mapper`, `orchestration` | GUI app-state construction, service orchestration, path access, and provider registry options construction. |\n26: | `src-tauri/src/commands/provider_settings.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Provider settings command host options construction, command orchestration, legacy TOML packaging/parsing, DTO/error mapping, and command validation. |\n27: | `src-tauri/src/commands/session_locate_export/orchestration.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `validator` | Session locate/export orchestration, environment loading, provider registry construction, output writing, and error/exit mapping. |\n28: | `src-tauri/src/run/balancing/finalization.rs` | `accessor`, `filter`, `mapper`, `orchestration` | Completed attempt finalization, artifact/session ingestion, external-session filtering, external session id access, and runtime cwd recording. |\n29: | `src-tauri/src/run/resume/orchestration.rs` | `accessor`, `filter`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `predicate`, `validator` | Headless resume target preparation, provider-ref resume execution, target validation, migration filtering, provider-ref predicates, and failure formatting. |\n30: | `src-tauri/src/wiring.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration` | Production and CLI default service graph construction, runtime path mapping, config loading, and directory error formatting. |\n31: | `src-tauri/tests/age244_s7b_production_wiring_source_guard.rs` | `accessor`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `parser`, `validator` | Source guard proving production registry constructors use process `PATH` entries through source reads, marker parsing, compact mapping, and assertions. |\n32: | `src-tauri/tests/s10_external_provider_resume.rs` | `accessor`, `filter`, `formatter`, `mapper`, `orchestration`, `parser`, `predicate`, `validator` | Full CLI launch/resume fixture proving external provider resume continuity through fixture construction, command orchestration, record parsing/filtering, predicates, and persisted invocation row assertions. |\n33: \n34: ## Production function inventory\n35: \n36: Only added or meaningfully changed production functions and production DTOs are listed.\n37: \n38: ### `crates/oulipoly-provider/src/generated.rs`\n39: \n40: | Function or symbol | A1 class | Meaning | Risk |\n41: |---|---|---|---|\n42: | `TrueBool::deserialize` | `parser` | Parses success envelope booleans through a separated protocol-bool parser and `ok=true` validator. | None; behavior remains the protocol invariant. |\n43: | `FalseBool::deserialize` | `parser` | Parses error envelope booleans through a separated protocol-bool parser and `ok=false` validator. | None; behavior remains the protocol invariant. |\n44: | `deserialize_protocol_bool` | `parser` | Parses provider protocol boolean fields before invariant validation. | None. |\n45: | `true_bool_from_value` | `validator` | Validates `ok=true` for success envelopes. | None. |\n46: | `false_bool_from_value` | `validator` | Validates `ok=false` for error envelopes. | None. |\n47: | `ProviderConcurrency` | `mapper` | Maps provider `describe.concurrency` JSON into a typed DTO while accepting optional legacy fields and flattened provider-specific metadata. | None; DTO broadening is bounded to this protocol object. |\n48: | `ErrorResponseEnvelope::process_status` | `mapper` | Maps provider error envelope process status into the typed `ProcessStatus` DTO. | None; raw JSON status parsing is removed from provider settings. |\n49: | `fixed_str_type!::*::deserialize` | `parser` | Parses fixed-kind launch event discriminator strings via a shared fixed-string validator. | None; ex